.\n   - Frame 18: Black dog and another dog on pedestals.\n   - Frame 19: Black dog standing, other dogs on pedestals.\n   - Frame 20: Dog visible behind trainer.\n   - Frame 21–22: Black dog standing on hind legs, interacting with trainer.\n   - Frame 25: Black dog standing on hind legs next to trainer.\n   - Frame 26–27: Wide shots showing multiple dogs on stage.\n   - Frame 28–30: Close-up showing 5+ dogs on pedestals, including large fluffy dogs and smaller breeds.\n\n2. **Multiple dogs simultaneously on stage**:  \n   In nearly every frame from 1 to 30, **at least 3–5 dogs are visible on stage at the same time**.\n\n3. **Dogs entering or exiting the stage**:  \n   While not explicitly shown entering from off-stage in these frames, the movement of dogs across the stage (e.g., Frame 2, 7, 12, 13, 21) implies dynamic performance, and the presence of multiple dogs in different positions across frames suggests they are actively performing rather than static props.\n\n4. **Dogs performing tricks or actions**:  \n   - Jumping (Frame 3, 12, 17, 21, 25)\n   - Standing on hind legs (Frame 17, 21, 22, 25)\n   - Balancing on pedestals (Frame 3, 6, 16, 18, 28–30)\n   - Running and following trainer (Frame 7, 8, 13)\n\n5. **Stage setting**:  \n   The stage has a pink floor and candy-themed backdrop (lollipops, ice cream, gumdrops), which is characteristic of “Britain’s Got Talent” set designs. While the logo is not visible, the judges’ table (Frame 10, 11) with the “X” lights is consistent with the show’s branding.\n\n6. **Trainer/owner present**:  \n   A young girl in a pink costume is clearly visible directing and interacting with the dogs in nearly every frame (e.g., Frame 3, 6, 7, 8, 12, 16, 17, 18, 19, 21, 22, 25, 28, 29, 30).\n\n---\n\n**Conclusion**:  \nThe claim that “0 dogs go on the stage in the first hour” is **factually incorrect**. Based on the visual evidence in these 30 frames alone, **at least 5–8 distinct dogs are visible performing on stage**, with multiple